00:16:15,740 --> 00:16:19,540
don't start writing at the beginning, start writing it wherever the end of the file is now.

169
00:16:20,100 --> 00:16:28,760
So all those can be done, and this is described in the book, by using the right set of names of constants

170
00:16:28,760 --> 00:16:34,150
and oaring them together. And that will return something that's called a file descriptor.

171
00:16:34,790 --> 00:16:39,430
And that's a very important idea that, and one of the reasons why we're talking about this today,

172
00:16:39,430 --> 00:16:43,830
this is going to be very important in your lab, is the notion of a file descriptor,

173
00:16:43,890 --> 00:16:53,470
which is just a small integer that lists which particular one of the open files that's being used by this particular

174
00:16:53,470 --> 00:16:55,990
program are you referring to?

175
00:16:56,730 --> 00:17:03,230
And so it's a small number because these are assigned sequentially when you begin your program

176
00:17:03,230 --> 00:17:09,280
and you run, and you're in fact limited on most machines to some

177
00:17:09,280 --> 00:17:13,740
some bounded number of open files you're allowed to have at any time.

