00:18:42,970 --> 00:18:47,030
would rather not because often there's an error and you deciding what you're going

193
00:18:47,030 --> 00:18:50,950
to do when there's an error is harder than then figuring just ignoring it all

194
00:18:50,950 --> 00:18:54,740
together and so there's a lot of code out there where people

195
00:18:54,760 --> 00:18:59,390
just cross their fingers and hope there's never errors in their system calls.

196
00:18:59,390 --> 00:19:05,390
But we're going to really push on you hard to say, every time you make a system call, check the return code,

197
00:19:05,390 --> 00:19:09,630
and do something if it's not right.

198
00:19:09,630 --> 00:19:16,430
And so the open will return a number, it's an int that it returns, but if it's a negative number,

199
00:19:16,430 --> 00:19:20,330
that means it couldn't open the file.

200
00:19:20,330 --> 00:19:25,510
For example, the file doesn't exist, or you're trying to open it in a mode that

201
00:19:25,530 --> 00:19:31,000
that you're not authorized to open it in.

202
00:19:31,000 --> 00:19:34,300
So there's three particular file descriptors

203
00:19:34,300 --> 00:19:37,660
associated with every single process that runs.

204
00:19:37,660 --> 00:19:40,400
One is standard input, the other is standard output, the other is standard output,

205
00:19:40,400 --> 00:19:42,300
and the third is standard error.

206
00:19:42,300 --> 00:19:46,500
And they always have file descriptor number 0, 1, and 2.

